IMPORT prelude

-- Coroners Act 2009 (NSW) -- the provisions the family of a person who has died meets, from the seat of that family
-- member (a relative, or the senior next of kin).
--
-- SEAT: the family member of a person whose death is (or may be) examined by a coroner -- the spouse, the parent, the
-- adult child, the brother or sister, the executor -- who may later bring or answer a claim arising from the death and
-- who meets the coronial process first. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Am I the senior next of
-- kin?", "Is this a reportable death?", "Must there be an inquest?", "Must the coroner let me appear?", "Can I object to
-- the post mortem, and by when must I go to the Supreme Court?", "May I bury the body?", "Can a witness's certified
-- evidence be used in my claim?". The coroner, the State Coroner, the Minister and the Supreme Court get no seat: what
-- they must or may do is encoded as what it means for the family member.
--
-- Provisions: ss 4 (spouse), 5 (relative), 6 (reportable death), 6A (senior next of kin); Chapter 3 Part 3.1 and Part
-- 3.2 (ss 18, 19, 21-29: jurisdiction, dispensing, inquests required); s 35 (duty to report), s 38 (death certificates);
-- ss 54, 56, 57, 60, 61(7), 65 (notice, the body, leave to appear, witnesses, certified evidence, the coroner's file);
-- s 75 (self-inflicted deaths: non-publication); ss 79, 81, 83 (resuming a suspended inquest, findings, fresh inquests);
-- ss 84-87 (Supreme Court orders); ss 89(6), 90(4)-(6), 91, 94, 95-99 (post mortems, whole organs, exhumation,
-- protection, objections); ss 100-101 (disposal of remains); the maximum penalties of the offences an individual can
-- commit in the Chapters encoded, with s 44 (ss 35, 44, 53, 62, 74, 75, 76, 100, 103A; not s 101M, in Chapter 9A, which
-- is out). What is out, and why, is in NOTES.md.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" or "is"
-- definition, a condition introduced by "if", "unless" or "only if", a flat "must not" or "cannot", a period the text
-- fixes), or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
-- Judgments the Act leaves to the coroner or the Supreme Court ("in the opinion of the coroner", "if satisfied",
-- "appears to the coroner") are asked as inputs and the answer is conditional on them.
--
-- Dates use only the built-in date functions (DATE_FROM_DMY, DATE_YEAR, DATE_MONTH, DATE_DAY, DATE_SERIAL), not the
-- daydate library.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 2009`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The family member's relationship to the person who died, as ss 5, 6A and 75(3) name them. A child includes the
-- children s 5(2) lists (children of a de facto or domestic relationship), and a parent the parents s 5(3) adds.
DECLARE `My relationship to the person who died` IS ONE OF
    `spouse or de facto partner`
    `parent`
    `person standing in loco parentis`
    `guardian`
    `child`
    `brother or sister`
    `executor named in the will`
    `legal personal representative immediately before the death`
    `another relationship`

-- s 6(1): the circumstances that make a death reportable, with the s 6(3) carve-out for excluded procedures. One answer:
-- the first that fits.
DECLARE `How the person died` IS ONE OF
    `a violent or unnatural death`
    `a sudden death, cause unknown`
    `under suspicious or unusual circumstances`
    `not the reasonably expected outcome of a health-related procedure`
    `not the reasonably expected outcome of a procedure the regulations exclude`
    `while a patient in (or temporarily absent from) a declared mental health facility`
    `an expected or natural death`

-- s 18: the connection with New South Wales. One answer: the first that fits.
DECLARE `Connection with New South Wales` IS ONE OF
    `the remains are in New South Wales`
    `the death, or its cause, happened in New South Wales`
    `the person was ordinarily resident in New South Wales`
    `the person was on a journey to or from New South Wales`
    `the person was last in New South Wales before the circumstances of the death arose`
    `no connection`

-- ss 23 and 24(1): the circumstances that give the State Coroner and the Deputy State Coroners (the senior coroners)
-- exclusive jurisdiction. One answer: the first that fits.
DECLARE `Custody, care or disability circumstance` IS ONE OF
    `in police or other lawful custody`
    `escaping or trying to escape lawful custody`
    `as a result of a police operation (not search and rescue)`
    `an inmate in (or temporarily absent from) a detention centre, correctional centre or lock-up`
    `on the way to be admitted to such a place, in the company of police or an official`
    `a child in care`
    `a child about whom a risk-of-harm report was made under the Care Act`
    `a child whose brother or sister was the subject of such a report`
    `a child whose death is or may be due to abuse or neglect, or is suspicious`
    `living in (or temporarily absent from) specialist disability accommodation or an assisted boarding house`
    `a person in the relevant group receiving prescribed assistance to live independently`
    `none of these circumstances`

-- The answer to "which coroner can hold an inquest?" (ss 18, 19, 21-24).
DECLARE `Who can hold the inquest` IS ONE OF
    `only a senior coroner (the State Coroner or a Deputy State Coroner)`
    `any coroner`
    `no coroner has jurisdiction`

-- The answer to "must there be an inquest?" (ss 25, 27, 28, 29, 87).
DECLARE `Whether an inquest is required` IS ONE OF
    `an inquest must be held`
    `an inquest is not required, and the coroner may dispense with it`
    `no coroner has jurisdiction to hold one`

-- s 26.
DECLARE `Reasons for dispensing` IS ONE OF
    `the coroner must give me written reasons for dispensing with the inquest`
    `the coroner must give me written reasons for refusing to give reasons`
    `the coroner need not give me reasons`
    `section 26 does not arise`

-- s 54.
DECLARE `Notice of the inquest` IS ONE OF
    `the coroner must give me the time and place`
    `the coroner may give me the time and place`
    `the Act does not settle it`

-- s 57.
DECLARE `Leave to appear` IS ONE OF
    `the coroner must grant me leave to appear or be represented`
    `the coroner may refuse me leave: exceptional circumstances`
    `the coroner may grant me leave: sufficient interest`
    `the Act gives me no leave on these facts`

-- ss 96, 98, 99.
DECLARE `How I can object` IS ONE OF
    `as senior next of kin, by written notice: ss 96 and 97 apply`
    `as senior next of kin, by written notice, but the coroner may refuse it because I have been or may be charged`
    `as the senior next of kin's authorised person, by written notice with a copy of my authority: ss 96 and 97 apply`
    `only as another person, under s 99: the 48-hour notice and the Supreme Court route do not apply`

-- s 97.
DECLARE `My Supreme Court application` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the coroner's direction or order is stayed until the Court decides`
    `out of time: the 48 hours after the notice have passed`
    `section 97 does not arise: no section 96(3) notice was given to me`

-- What the Supreme Court has done on an application under s 97.
DECLARE `Supreme Court order on my application` IS ONE OF
    `no order yet`
    `an order that the post mortem not be conducted`
    `an order that it be conducted within limitations`
    `an order confirming the coroner's direction`

-- ss 96, 97.
DECLARE `Can the post mortem go ahead` IS ONE OF
    `yes`
    `yes, but only within the limitations the Supreme Court set`
    `not yet: the time the notice gives (at least 48 hours after it) has not come`
    `not yet: the coroner must first decide on my objection and give me written notice`
    `not while my Supreme Court application is pending: it is stayed`
    `no: the Supreme Court ordered that it not be conducted`
    `nothing in Part 8.2 holds it back: no senior next of kin objected`

-- s 79.
DECLARE `Why the inquest was suspended` IS ONE OF
    `a person was charged with an indictable offence about the death (s 78(1)(a))`
    `the coroner formed the opinion that a known person committed an indictable offence (s 78(1)(b))`

DECLARE `Where the charge stands` IS ONE OF
    `not finally determined`
    `the person was discharged`
    `no further appeal can be made without an extension of time`
    `the Attorney General or the DPP directed that no further proceedings be taken`

-- s 61(7).
DECLARE `The later proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a civil proceeding in a NSW court`
    `a criminal proceeding in a NSW court, other than for the falsity of the evidence`
    `a criminal proceeding for the falsity of that evidence`
    `a hearing before a person or body authorised by NSW law, or by consent, to take evidence`
    `a proceeding outside NSW`

-- s 100.
DECLARE `What I want to do with the remains` IS ONE OF
    `bury or cremate them, or place them in a permanent resting place`
    `hand them over for anatomical or medical research`
    `take them out of New South Wales (not cremated remains)`
    `take cremated remains out of New South Wales`

DECLARE `Disposal document` IS ONE OF
    `a doctor's notice under s 39 of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act`
    `a doctor's certificate or notice under s 12(3) of that Act (stillborn child)`
    `a coroner's order under s 101`
    `a Registrar's certificate under s 51 of that Act`
    `no document`

-- The offences an individual can commit under the Act.
DECLARE `Offence` IS ONE OF
    `failing to report a reportable death (s 35(2))`
    `obstructing a person executing a coronial investigation scene order (s 44)`
    `failing to comply with a notice to produce (s 53(5))`
    `refusing to be sworn, examined, answer or produce (s 62)`
    `contravening an order clearing the court or forbidding publication (s 74(3))`
    `publishing in breach of a self-inflicted-death order or s 75(5) (s 75(7))`
    `publishing a disallowed question, warning, objection or submission (s 76)`
    `burying or cremating remains without authority (s 100(1))`
    `handing over or removing remains without authority (s 100(2))`
    `disrespectful behaviour in coronial proceedings (s 103A)`

DECLARE `Maximum penalty` HAS
    `penalty units` IS A NUMBER
    `months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER
    `days of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER
    `fine and imprisonment both` IS A BOOLEAN

-- The parties and acts of the one regulative rule (s 97(1)).
DECLARE `Post mortem party` IS ONE OF `the senior next of kin`
DECLARE `Post mortem act` IS ONE OF `apply to the Supreme Court`

§§ `Dates`

-- A date falls within the last N years before a later date: on or after the same calendar day N years earlier. Worked
-- on year, month and day so that 29 February never produces an invalid date.
GIVEN `the earlier day` IS A DATE, `the years` IS A NUMBER, `the later day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the earlier day` `falls within` `the years` `years before` `the later day` IF
        DATE_SERIAL `the earlier day` AT MOST DATE_SERIAL `the later day`
    AND (    DATE_YEAR `the earlier day` GREATER THAN (DATE_YEAR `the later day` MINUS `the years`)
         OR (    DATE_YEAR `the earlier day` EQUALS (DATE_YEAR `the later day` MINUS `the years`)
             AND (    DATE_MONTH `the earlier day` GREATER THAN DATE_MONTH `the later day`
                  OR (    DATE_MONTH `the earlier day` EQUALS DATE_MONTH `the later day`
                      AND DATE_DAY `the earlier day` AT LEAST DATE_DAY `the later day`))))

§§ `s 4, s 5 -- spouse and relative`

-- s 4 "spouse means (a) the person to whom a person is legally married ... or (b) a de facto partner, but where more than
-- one person would so qualify as a spouse, means only the last person so to qualify."
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died`
      `someone later qualified as the spouse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `I am the spouse` `my relationship` `someone later qualified as the spouse` IF
        `my relationship` EQUALS `spouse or de facto partner`
    AND (NOT `someone later qualified as the spouse`)

-- s 5(1) "a relative ... is an adult who is (a) the spouse ..., a parent ..., a person who stands in loco parentis ...,
-- a guardian ... or a child of that person, or (b) if there is no relative, as defined in paragraph (a), of that
-- person -- a brother or sister of that person." An "is" definition with an "if": when it fails, JUST FALSE.
@export Am I a "relative" of the person who died under the Coroners Act (s 5)? Relatives must be given leave to appear at the inquest (s 57(3)) and can stop a doctor certifying some deaths (s 38(3)).
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "spouse or de facto partner", "parent", "person standing in loco parentis", "guardian", "child" (including a child of a de facto or domestic relationship, s 5(2)), "brother or sister", "executor named in the will", "legal personal representative immediately before the death", "another relationship"
      `I am 18 or older` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 18 years old or older (an adult)
      `someone later qualified as the spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are the spouse or de facto partner: whether someone else became the person's spouse or de facto partner after you (only the last one counts, s 4). Otherwise false
      `the person had an adult spouse, parent, guardian, child or person in loco parentis` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has an adult spouse (the last one), parent, guardian, child, or person who stood in loco parentis to them (other than you)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a relative` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am 18 or older`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `first group`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE IF     `my relationship` EQUALS `brother or sister`
                 AND (NOT `the person had an adult spouse, parent, guardian, child or person in loco parentis`)
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `first group` MEANS
               `I am the spouse` `my relationship` `someone later qualified as the spouse`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `parent`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `person standing in loco parentis`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `guardian`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `child`

§§ `s 6A -- senior next of kin`

-- s 6A(1): (a) the spouse; (b) if no spouse or a spouse is not available -- any adult child; (c) if no spouse or child
-- or a spouse or child is not available -- either parent; (d) if no spouse, child or living parent (or not available)
-- -- any adult brother or sister; (e) if no spouse, child, living parent, brother or sister (or not available) -- an
-- executor named in the will, or the legal personal representative immediately before the death. An "is" definition:
-- JUST FALSE where it fails. Paragraphs (c) and (e) are barred by a "child" or a "brother or sister" of any age, but
-- (b) and (d) only make adults senior next of kin; whether a child or sibling under 18 is "available" is not said
-- (fork F2): where the answer turns on it, NOTHING.
-- s 6A(2): the coroner may treat the legal personal representative as senior next of kin if satisfied that the person
-- available to act is unable to do so (an input).
@export Am I the "senior next of kin" of the person who died (s 6A)? The senior next of kin can object to a post mortem and go to the Supreme Court (ss 96-97), is consulted before an inquest or post mortem is dispensed with (ss 25(2), 89(6)), and is told if a whole organ is kept (s 90(6)).
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "spouse or de facto partner", "parent", "person standing in loco parentis", "guardian", "child", "brother or sister", "executor named in the will", "legal personal representative immediately before the death", "another relationship"
      `I am 18 or older` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 18 years old or older
      `someone later qualified as the spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are the spouse or de facto partner: whether someone else became the person's spouse or de facto partner after you (only the last one counts, s 4). Otherwise false
      `a spouse is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has a spouse (the last one), other than you, who is available to act
      `an adult child is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has a child aged 18 or over, other than you, who is available to act
      `the person had a child under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has a child under 18 (other than you)
      `a parent is living and available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a parent of the person who died, other than you, is living and available to act
      `an adult brother or sister is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has a brother or sister aged 18 or over, other than you, who is available to act
      `the person had a brother or sister under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died has a brother or sister under 18 (other than you)
      `the coroner treats me as senior next of kin under s 6A(2)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you were the legal personal representative: whether the coroner, satisfied that the person available to act as senior next of kin is unable to, has decided to treat you as senior next of kin (s 6A(2)). Otherwise false
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I the senior next of kin` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I am the spouse` `my relationship` `someone later qualified as the spouse` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF     `my relationship` EQUALS `legal personal representative immediately before the death`
              AND `the coroner treats me as senior next of kin under s 6A(2)` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `a spouse is available` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `my relationship` EQUALS `child`
           THEN IF `I am 18 or older` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
           IF `an adult child is available` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `my relationship` EQUALS `parent`
           THEN IF `the person had a child under 18`
                THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: whether a child under 18 is "available" under s 6A(1)(c); fork F2)
                ELSE JUST TRUE
           IF `a parent is living and available` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `my relationship` EQUALS `brother or sister`
           THEN IF `I am 18 or older`
                THEN IF `the person had a child under 18`
                     THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: whether a child under 18 is "available"; fork F2)
                     ELSE JUST TRUE
                ELSE JUST FALSE
           IF `an adult brother or sister is available` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF    `my relationship` EQUALS `executor named in the will`
              OR `my relationship` EQUALS `legal personal representative immediately before the death`
           THEN IF `the person had a child under 18` OR `the person had a brother or sister under 18`
                THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: whether a child or sibling under 18 is "available" under s 6A(1)(e); fork F2)
                ELSE JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 6 -- reportable death`

-- s 6(1) "a person's death is a reportable death if the death occurs in any of the following circumstances" (a)-(c),
-- (e), (f); (d) is repealed. s 6(3): "health-related procedure ... does not include any procedure of a kind prescribed by
-- the regulations as being an excluded procedure" (the regulations are not deposited: asked). An "if" list: JUST FALSE
-- when none fits.
@export Is the death a "reportable death" that must go to the coroner (s 6)?
GIVEN `how the person died` IS A `How the person died` @desc How the person died; pick the first that fits. One of: "a violent or unnatural death", "a sudden death, cause unknown", "under suspicious or unusual circumstances", "not the reasonably expected outcome of a health-related procedure" (medical, surgical, dental, anaesthetic or drug), "not the reasonably expected outcome of a procedure the regulations exclude", "while a patient in (or temporarily absent from) a declared mental health facility", "an expected or natural death"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a reportable death` MEANS
    IF `is reportable` `how the person died` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

GIVEN `how the person died` IS A `How the person died`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is reportable` `how the person died` IF
    CONSIDER `how the person died`
    WHEN `not the reasonably expected outcome of a procedure the regulations exclude` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an expected or natural death` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `ss 18, 19, 21-24 -- who can hold an inquest`

-- s 24(3) "child means a person who is less than 18 years old"; "child in care" likewise. s 24(1)(b), (c): a report
-- "within the period of 3 years immediately preceding the child's death". A report made on the same calendar day 3
-- years before the death is taken as within the period (fork F17).
GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance`
      `age at death` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the report` IS A DATE
      `the day of death` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `gives a senior coroner jurisdiction` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report` `the day of death` IF
    CONSIDER `the circumstance`
    WHEN `none of these circumstances` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a child in care` THEN `a child`
    WHEN `a child about whom a risk-of-harm report was made under the Care Act` THEN `a child` AND `reported in time`
    WHEN `a child whose brother or sister was the subject of such a report` THEN `a child` AND `reported in time`
    WHEN `a child whose death is or may be due to abuse or neglect, or is suspicious` THEN `a child`
    OTHERWISE TRUE
    WHERE
        `a child` MEANS `age at death` LESS THAN 18
        `reported in time` MEANS `the day of the report` `falls within` 3 `years before` `the day of death`

-- ss 23 (custody, police operations) only: s 27(1)(b) makes an inquest compulsory for these.
GIVEN `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a section 23 circumstance` `the circumstance` IF
    CONSIDER `the circumstance`
    WHEN `in police or other lawful custody` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `escaping or trying to escape lawful custody` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `as a result of a police operation (not search and rescue)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an inmate in (or temporarily absent from) a detention centre, correctional centre or lock-up` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `on the way to be admitted to such a place, in the company of police or an official` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 18(1) "A coroner does not have jurisdiction ... unless it appears to the coroner that" the remains are in the State,
-- the death or its cause occurred in the State, or the person had a sufficient connection (s 18(2)). s 19: none
-- "unless ... the death ... occurred within the last 100 years". s 21(1): jurisdiction "if" the death is (or may be)
-- reportable or no doctor has (or may have) certified the cause. ss 22-24: a senior coroner's exclusive jurisdiction,
-- and s 22(2) where both arise only a senior coroner may hold it. A day of death exactly 100 years before is taken as
-- within (fork F3).
GIVEN `connection` IS A `Connection with New South Wales`
      `the day of death` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
      `how the person died` IS A `How the person died`
      `a doctor has certified the cause of death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance`
      `age at death` IS A NUMBER
      `the day of the report` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Who can hold the inquest`
`who can hold it` `connection` `the day of death` `today` `how the person died` `a doctor has certified the cause of death` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `connection` EQUALS `no connection` THEN `no coroner has jurisdiction`
           IF (NOT (`the day of death` `falls within` 100 `years before` `today`)) THEN `no coroner has jurisdiction`
           IF `gives a senior coroner jurisdiction` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report` `the day of death`
           THEN `only a senior coroner (the State Coroner or a Deputy State Coroner)`
           IF `is reportable` `how the person died` OR (NOT `a doctor has certified the cause of death`) THEN `any coroner`
           OTHERWISE `no coroner has jurisdiction`

@export Which coroner, if any, can hold an inquest into the death (ss 18, 19, 21-24)? The answer assumes the facts appear to the coroner as you state them.
GIVEN `connection` IS A `Connection with New South Wales` @desc The person's connection with NSW; pick the first that fits. One of: "the remains are in New South Wales", "the death, or its cause, happened in New South Wales", "the person was ordinarily resident in New South Wales", "the person was on a journey to or from New South Wales", "the person was last in New South Wales before the circumstances of the death arose", "no connection"
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (or is suspected to have died)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (the inquest must concern a death within the last 100 years)
      `how the person died` IS A `How the person died` @desc How the person died; pick the first that fits. One of: "a violent or unnatural death", "a sudden death, cause unknown", "under suspicious or unusual circumstances", "not the reasonably expected outcome of a health-related procedure", "not the reasonably expected outcome of a procedure the regulations exclude", "while a patient in (or temporarily absent from) a declared mental health facility", "an expected or natural death"
      `a doctor has certified the cause of death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a doctor (or, for a death outside NSW, a person entitled there) has given a certificate as to the cause of death
      `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance` @desc Whether the person died in one of these circumstances; pick the first that fits. One of: "in police or other lawful custody", "escaping or trying to escape lawful custody", "as a result of a police operation (not search and rescue)", "an inmate in (or temporarily absent from) a detention centre, correctional centre or lock-up", "on the way to be admitted to such a place, in the company of police or an official", "a child in care", "a child about whom a risk-of-harm report was made under the Care Act", "a child whose brother or sister was the subject of such a report", "a child whose death is or may be due to abuse or neglect, or is suspicious", "living in (or temporarily absent from) specialist disability accommodation or an assisted boarding house", "a person in the relevant group receiving prescribed assistance to live independently", "none of these circumstances"
      `age at death` IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in whole years when they died
      `the day of the report` IS A DATE @desc For a child about whom (or whose brother or sister) a risk-of-significant-harm report was made under Part 2 of Chapter 3 of the Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection) Act 1998: the day of the latest report. Otherwise any day
GIVETH A `Who can hold the inquest`
`Which coroner can hold an inquest` MEANS
    `who can hold it` `connection` `the day of death` `today` `how the person died` `a doctor has certified the cause of death` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report`

-- s 24(2): "If jurisdiction to hold an inquest concerning the death of a child arises under this section or section 23,
-- the senior coroner must use his or her best endeavours to notify the following persons of any right that they have to
-- legal representation at the inquest -- (a) the persons having parental responsibility for the child, (b) the child's
-- parents (if they do not have that responsibility), (c) such family members as would be reasonably expected to have an
-- immediate interest in the outcome of the inquest." The duty is the senior coroner's; the family member asks whether it
-- is owed to them. The "if" fails (no child, no s 23 or s 24 jurisdiction): JUST FALSE. A person outside the list: the
-- list is not closed with "only", so NOTHING (fork F13). Whether a family member "would be reasonably expected to have an
-- immediate interest" is the senior coroner's judgment, asked as an input.
DECLARE `My position towards the child` IS ONE OF
    `a person with parental responsibility for the child`
    `a parent without parental responsibility`
    `a family member reasonably expected to have an immediate interest in the outcome`
    `none of these`

@export My child (or a child in my family) died: must the senior coroner try to tell me of any right I have to a lawyer at the inquest (s 24(2))? The duty is to use "best endeavours" to notify you.
GIVEN `connection` IS A `Connection with New South Wales` @desc The child's connection with NSW; pick the first that fits. One of: "the remains are in New South Wales", "the death, or its cause, happened in New South Wales", "the person was ordinarily resident in New South Wales", "the person was on a journey to or from New South Wales", "the person was last in New South Wales before the circumstances of the death arose", "no connection"
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the child died (or is suspected to have died)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance` @desc Whether the child died in one of the custody, care or disability circumstances of ss 23 and 24; pick the first that fits, or "none of these circumstances"
      `age at death` IS A NUMBER @desc The child's age in whole years at death (s 24(3): a child is under 18)
      `the day of the report` IS A DATE @desc For a child about whom (or whose brother or sister) a risk-of-significant-harm report was made: the day of the latest report. Otherwise any day
      `my position` IS A `My position towards the child` @desc Your position. One of: "a person with parental responsibility for the child", "a parent without parental responsibility", "a family member reasonably expected to have an immediate interest in the outcome" (the senior coroner's judgment), "none of these"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the senior coroner try to tell me of my right to a lawyer` MEANS
    IF NOT (    (NOT (`connection` EQUALS `no connection`))
            AND `the day of death` `falls within` 100 `years before` `today`
            AND `age at death` LESS THAN 18
            AND `gives a senior coroner jurisdiction` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report` `the day of death`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `my position` EQUALS `none of these`
         THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a list left open: s 24(2) names whom the senior coroner must try to notify, without "only"; fork F13)
         ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 25, 27-29, 87 -- must there be an inquest`

-- s 27(1): an inquest "is required to be held" if (a) homicide (not suicide) appears, (b) the jurisdiction arises under
-- s 23, (c) it is not sufficiently disclosed whether the person died, or their identity and the date and place of death,
-- (d) the manner and cause of death are not sufficiently disclosed (unless the inquest was suspended or continued under
-- s 78). s 27(2): not required under s 27 if an inquest or official inquiry has been or is to be held outside the State.
-- s 28: required if the Minister or State Coroner directs; s 29: the State Coroner may direct an inquest after a
-- coroner dispensed. s 87(2), (3): a Supreme Court order gives jurisdiction and the coroner "may not dispense".
-- s 25(1): otherwise the coroner "may dispense with the inquest unless an inquest is required".
@export Must there be an inquest into the death, or may the coroner dispense with it (ss 25, 27, 28, 29, 87)? The answer assumes the facts appear to the coroner as you state them.
GIVEN `connection` IS A `Connection with New South Wales` @desc The person's connection with NSW; pick the first that fits. One of: "the remains are in New South Wales", "the death, or its cause, happened in New South Wales", "the person was ordinarily resident in New South Wales", "the person was on a journey to or from New South Wales", "the person was last in New South Wales before the circumstances of the death arose", "no connection"
      `the day of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (or is suspected to have died)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `how the person died` IS A `How the person died` @desc How the person died; pick the first that fits. One of: "a violent or unnatural death", "a sudden death, cause unknown", "under suspicious or unusual circumstances", "not the reasonably expected outcome of a health-related procedure", "not the reasonably expected outcome of a procedure the regulations exclude", "while a patient in (or temporarily absent from) a declared mental health facility", "an expected or natural death"
      `a doctor has certified the cause of death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a doctor has given a certificate as to the cause of death
      `the circumstance` IS A `Custody, care or disability circumstance` @desc Whether the person died in one of the custody, care or disability circumstances of ss 23 and 24; pick the first that fits, or "none of these circumstances"
      `age at death` IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in whole years when they died
      `the day of the report` IS A DATE @desc For a child about whom (or whose brother or sister) a risk-of-significant-harm report was made: the day of the latest report. Otherwise any day
      `it may have been homicide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears that the person died or might have died as a result of homicide (not suicide)
      `it is not clear whether the person died, or who, when and where` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it has not been sufficiently disclosed whether the person died, or their identity and the date and place of death
      `the manner and cause of death are not clear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the manner and cause of death have not been sufficiently disclosed
      `the inquest was suspended or continued under s 78` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest into the death was suspended or continued under s 78 (a person charged with, or who may have committed, an indictable offence)
      `an inquest or official inquiry outside NSW` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest or other official inquiry into the death has been held, or is to be held, outside NSW
      `the Minister or State Coroner directed an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister or the State Coroner has directed that an inquest be held (ss 28, 29)
      `the Supreme Court ordered an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that an inquest (or a fresh inquest) be held (ss 84, 85, 87)
GIVETH A `Whether an inquest is required`
`Must an inquest be held` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Supreme Court ordered an inquest` THEN `an inquest must be held`
           IF `who can hold it` `connection` `the day of death` `today` `how the person died` `a doctor has certified the cause of death` `the circumstance` `age at death` `the day of the report` EQUALS `no coroner has jurisdiction`
           THEN `no coroner has jurisdiction to hold one`
           IF `the Minister or State Coroner directed an inquest` THEN `an inquest must be held`
           IF `an inquest or official inquiry outside NSW` THEN `an inquest is not required, and the coroner may dispense with it`
           IF    `it may have been homicide`
              OR `is a section 23 circumstance` `the circumstance`
              OR `it is not clear whether the person died, or who, when and where`
              OR (`the manner and cause of death are not clear` AND (NOT `the inquest was suspended or continued under s 78`))
           THEN `an inquest must be held`
           OTHERWISE `an inquest is not required, and the coroner may dispense with it`

-- s 25(3): "A coroner who has previously dispensed with the holding of an inquest ... may subsequently hold an inquest
-- ... if the coroner is of the opinion that the discovery of new evidence or facts makes it necessary or desirable in the
-- interests of justice to hold the inquest." No earlier dispensing: s 25(3) does not arise, NOTHING. The "if" fails:
-- JUST FALSE, worded as "s 25(3) gives no power on these facts" (fork F14; ss 29 and 84 remain).
@export The coroner dispensed with an inquest: can the coroner still hold one because new evidence or facts have come to light (s 25(3))? A "no" here is about s 25(3) only: the State Coroner may still direct an inquest (s 29), and the Supreme Court may order one (s 84).
GIVEN `the coroner dispensed with the inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has dispensed with an inquest into the death
      `the coroner considers new evidence makes an inquest necessary or desirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that the discovery of new evidence or facts makes it necessary or desirable in the interests of justice to hold the inquest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the coroner still hold the inquest it dispensed with` MEANS
    IF NOT `the coroner dispensed with the inquest`
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 25(3) speaks only of an inquest already dispensed with)
    ELSE IF `the coroner considers new evidence makes an inquest necessary or desirable` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 26 -- reasons for dispensing`

-- s 26(1): a coroner who dispenses "must, on request by ... any person who, in the opinion of the coroner, has a
-- sufficient interest", give written reasons. s 26(2): if refused for want of sufficient interest, the coroner "is
-- required, at the written request of the person, to give the person the reasons for the refusal".
@export The coroner has dispensed with an inquest: must the coroner give me written reasons (s 26)?
GIVEN `the coroner dispensed with the inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has dispensed with an inquest into the death
      `I asked for reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner for the reasons for dispensing with the inquest
      `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest of any kind in the circumstances of the death (a close relative usually will)
      `I asked in writing for the reasons for the refusal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the coroner refused because you lack a sufficient interest: whether you asked in writing for the reasons for that refusal
GIVETH A `Reasons for dispensing`
`Must the coroner give me reasons for dispensing` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `the coroner dispensed with the inquest`) THEN `section 26 does not arise`
           IF (NOT `I asked for reasons`) THEN `the coroner need not give me reasons`
           IF `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` THEN `the coroner must give me written reasons for dispensing with the inquest`
           IF `I asked in writing for the reasons for the refusal` THEN `the coroner must give me written reasons for refusing to give reasons`
           OTHERWISE `the coroner need not give me reasons`

§§ `s 35 -- the duty to report a death`

-- s 35(1) "This section applies to any person who has reasonable grounds to believe that a death ... (a) is a reportable
-- death or occurred in circumstances that would be examinable under Division 2 of Part 3.2, and (b) has not been reported
-- in accordance with subsection (2)." s 35(2) "must report ... to a police officer, a coroner or an assistant coroner as
-- soon as possible". Max penalty 10 penalty units. Where s 35(1) does not reach the person, JUST FALSE ("this section
-- puts no duty on you"; fork F4).
@export Must I report the death to the police or a coroner (s 35)? If so, it must be done as soon as possible after you have the grounds; the maximum penalty for not doing so is 10 penalty units.
GIVEN `I have reasonable grounds to believe it is reportable or in custody or care circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe the death is a reportable death (violent, unnatural, sudden and unexplained, suspicious, unexpected after a health procedure, or of a mental health patient) or happened in the custody, police, care or disability circumstances of ss 23 and 24
      `it has already been reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death has already been reported to a police officer, a coroner or an assistant coroner
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I report the death` MEANS
    IF     `I have reasonable grounds to believe it is reportable or in custody or care circumstances`
       AND (NOT `it has already been reported`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 38 -- death certificates`

-- s 38(1): a doctor "must not give a certificate as to the cause of death ... if the medical practitioner is of the
-- opinion that" the death is reportable or examinable by a senior coroner. s 38(2): "Despite subsection (1)" a doctor
-- may certify if of the opinion the person was 72 or older, died in circumstances other than s 6(1)(b)-(f), s 23 or
-- s 24(1), and died after an accident attributable to their age, that contributed substantially to the death and was
-- not caused by another person's act or omission. s 38(3): "may not" if a relative objects before the certificate.
-- Where the doctor is not of the opinion in s 38(1), s 38 does not speak: NOTHING.
@export May the doctor certify the cause of death, so that the death need not go to the coroner (s 38)? A relative's objection stops a certificate given under the 72-and-over exception.
GIVEN `the doctor thinks the death is reportable or in custody or care circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor is of the opinion that the death is a reportable death or happened in the custody, police, care or disability circumstances of ss 23 and 24
      `the doctor thinks the person was 72 or older` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor is of the opinion that the person was aged 72 or older
      `the only ground is a violent or unnatural death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the doctor's opinion, the death is reportable only because it was violent or unnatural (s 6(1)(a)): not sudden and unexplained, suspicious, after a health procedure, of a mental health patient, or in custody, care or disability circumstances
      `the doctor thinks an age-related accident caused it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the doctor is of the opinion that the person died after an injury from an accident that was attributable to their age, contributed substantially to the death, and was not caused by anyone else's act or omission
      `a relative objected before the certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a relative of the person (see the "relative" question) told the doctor they object to the certificate before it was given
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May the doctor certify the cause of death` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the doctor thinks the death is reportable or in custody or care circumstances`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 38 bars only where the doctor holds the s 38(1) opinion; the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act governs the certificate)
    ELSE IF     `the doctor thinks the person was 72 or older`
            AND `the only ground is a violent or unnatural death`
            AND `the doctor thinks an age-related accident caused it`
            AND (NOT `a relative objected before the certificate`)
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 54 -- notice of the inquest`

-- s 54(1): the coroner "(b) must give particulars of the time and place to any person who has given notice in writing
-- to the coroner of his or her intention to seek leave to appear or to be represented", "(c) ... must give particulars ...
-- to the person's next of kin if the coroner has been informed of the name and address of the next of kin", "(d) may
-- give particulars ... to any person who has, in the opinion of the coroner, a sufficient interest". "next of kin" is
-- not defined (only "senior next of kin" is): asked as a fact (finding 3).
@export Must the coroner tell me the time and place of the inquest (s 54)?
GIVEN `I gave written notice that I will seek leave to appear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have told the coroner in writing that you intend to seek leave to appear, or to be represented, at the inquest
      `I am next of kin and the coroner has my name and address` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the next of kin of the person who died and the coroner has been told your name and address
      `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest in the inquest
GIVETH A `Notice of the inquest`
`Must the coroner tell me when and where the inquest is` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I gave written notice that I will seek leave to appear` OR `I am next of kin and the coroner has my name and address`
           THEN `the coroner must give me the time and place`
           IF `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` THEN `the coroner may give me the time and place`
           OTHERWISE `the Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: s 54(1) neither requires nor forbids notice to others)

§§ `s 56 -- the coroner's right to the body`

-- s 56(1) "A coroner has a right to take possession of and retain the remains ... whenever the coroner has jurisdiction
-- to hold or is holding an inquest", and s 56(2) that right "has priority over any other right to possession". Where
-- there is no such jurisdiction, s 56 does not speak: NOTHING.
@export Does the coroner's right to take and keep the body come before my right to it (s 56)?
GIVEN `a coroner has jurisdiction to hold or is holding an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner has jurisdiction to hold, or is holding, an inquest into the death (see the "which coroner" question)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the coroner's right to the body come first` MEANS
    IF `a coroner has jurisdiction to hold or is holding an inquest`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 56 speaks only where the coroner has jurisdiction)

§§ `s 57 -- leave to appear`

-- s 57(1): the coroner "may grant leave to any person, who in the opinion of the coroner has a sufficient interest", to
-- appear or be represented by a lawyer. s 57(3): at an inquest the coroner "must grant leave ... to any person who is a
-- relative ... unless the coroner is satisfied that there are exceptional circumstances that justify ... refusing".
-- With leave: examine and cross-examine witnesses (s 57(2)), ask for a witness (s 60), ask for a suspended inquest to
-- resume (s 79(2)), apply for a fresh inquest (s 83(5)).
@export Will the coroner let me appear (or be represented by a lawyer) at the inquest (s 57)? With leave you can question witnesses, ask for a witness to be called, and later apply for a fresh inquest.
GIVEN `I am a relative` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a "relative" of the person who died under s 5 (see the "relative" question)
      `the coroner is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that exceptional circumstances justify refusing you leave
      `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is of the opinion that you have a sufficient interest in the subject-matter of the inquest
GIVETH A `Leave to appear`
`Will I be given leave to appear` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I am a relative` AND (NOT `the coroner is satisfied there are exceptional circumstances`)
           THEN `the coroner must grant me leave to appear or be represented`
           IF `I am a relative` THEN `the coroner may refuse me leave: exceptional circumstances`
           IF `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` THEN `the coroner may grant me leave: sufficient interest`
           OTHERWISE `the Act gives me no leave on these facts` -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own wording: s 57(1) gives leave only to a person with a sufficient interest, without "only")

§§ `s 60 -- asking for a witness`

-- s 60(1): "A person granted leave ... may apply to the coroner to have a particular person examined"; (2) "A coroner
-- who refuses such an application must give the applicant the reasons"; (3) it must be dealt with "before the
-- proceedings are concluded". Without leave, s 60 gives no application: NOTHING.
@export If the coroner refuses my application to have a particular witness examined, must the coroner give me reasons (s 60)?
GIVEN `I have leave to appear` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been granted leave to appear or be represented at the inquest
      `the coroner refused my application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner refused your application to have a particular person examined
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the coroner give me reasons for refusing my witness` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I have leave to appear`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 60 gives the application only to a person granted leave)
    ELSE IF `the coroner refused my application` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 61(7) -- certified evidence in a later claim`

-- s 61(7): evidence given under a s 61 certificate, and anything obtained in consequence, "cannot be used against the
-- person" in any proceeding in a NSW court or before a person or body authorised by NSW law (or by consent) to take
-- evidence; "However, this does not apply to a criminal proceeding in respect of the falsity of the evidence". A flat
-- "cannot": JUST FALSE. Outside its reach the Act is silent: NOTHING.
@export In my later claim, can evidence a witness gave at the inquest under a self-incrimination certificate be used against that witness (s 61(7))? This includes anything obtained because of that evidence.
GIVEN `the witness was given a s 61 certificate for that evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner gave the witness a certificate under s 61 for the evidence (or the evidence was obtained as a direct or indirect consequence of certified evidence)
      `the later proceeding` IS A `The later proceeding` @desc Where you want to use the evidence. One of: "a civil proceeding in a NSW court", "a criminal proceeding in a NSW court, other than for the falsity of the evidence", "a criminal proceeding for the falsity of that evidence", "a hearing before a person or body authorised by NSW law, or by consent, to take evidence", "a proceeding outside NSW"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can certified evidence be used against the witness` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the witness was given a s 61 certificate for that evidence`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 61(7) speaks only of certified evidence)
    ELSE CONSIDER `the later proceeding`
         WHEN `a criminal proceeding for the falsity of that evidence` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a limit in the text's own wording: s 61(7) "does not apply" to it, and says nothing more)
         WHEN `a proceeding outside NSW` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 61(7) reaches NSW courts and bodies only)
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 65 -- a copy of the coroner's file`

-- s 65(2): a copy is "to" be supplied "if (a) the coroner ... is satisfied that it is appropriate ... and (b) the person
-- pays the fee" (the Local Court copying fee, or a prescribed fee: not deposited). s 65(6): "is not to be supplied" in
-- contravention of a s 65(4) direction or a s 74 order. "if" and a flat negative: JUST FALSE.
@export Can I get a copy of the coroner's file, or the part I asked for (s 65)?
GIVEN `the coroner is satisfied access is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner (or assistant coroner) is satisfied that it is appropriate for you to have access, having regard to open justice, the impact on the relatives, your connection to the case and your reasons
      `I have paid the fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the copying fee (the Local Court fee for a copy of a document, or a fee set by the regulations)
      `the coroner directed that it not be supplied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has noted on the file a direction that the part you want is not to be supplied (s 65(4))
      `a s 74 order covers it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an order under s 74 (clearing the court, or forbidding publication of evidence or submissions) covers the part you want
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get a copy of the coroner's file` MEANS
    IF     `the coroner is satisfied access is appropriate`
       AND `I have paid the fee`
       AND (NOT `the coroner directed that it not be supplied`)
       AND (NOT `a s 74 order covers it`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 75 -- self-inflicted deaths`

-- s 75(1), (2)(b)(ii): if it appears to the coroner that a death is self-inflicted, a non-publication order may prohibit
-- publishing anything that identifies a person "as being a relative of a person whose death ... may have been
-- self-inflicted". s 75(3): "the following persons are relatives": spouse, parent, person in loco parentis, guardian,
-- child, a person living with them as spouse at the death, brother or sister -- with no age limit and no ranking, unlike
-- s 5 (finding 4). The order is the coroner's choice: JUST TRUE means it can reach the family member.
@export If the death may have been self-inflicted, can a coroner's non-publication order protect me from being identified as a relative (s 75(2)(b), (3))?
GIVEN `it appears to the coroner the death was self-inflicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the coroner that the death (or suspected death) was self-inflicted
      `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc Your relationship to the person who died. One of: "spouse or de facto partner", "parent", "person standing in loco parentis", "guardian", "child", "brother or sister", "executor named in the will", "legal personal representative immediately before the death", "another relationship"
      `I was living with them as their spouse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, at the death, you were living with the person as their spouse
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a non-publication order protect me` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it appears to the coroner the death was self-inflicted`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF    `a listed relative`
            OR `I was living with them as their spouse`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text lists without closing the list: s 75(3) says who "are relatives", not that no one else is)
    WHERE
        `a listed relative` MEANS
               `my relationship` EQUALS `spouse or de facto partner`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `parent`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `person standing in loco parentis`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `guardian`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `child`
            OR `my relationship` EQUALS `brother or sister`

-- s 75(5): "If a finding is made in an inquest to the effect that the death of a person was self-inflicted, a report of
-- the proceedings ... must not be published after the finding unless (and to the extent that) the coroner ... makes an
-- order permitting the publication". Without such a finding s 75(5) does not speak: NOTHING.
@export After an inquest finds the death was self-inflicted, may a report of the inquest be published (s 75(5))?
GIVEN `the inquest found the death self-inflicted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the inquest made a finding that the death was self-inflicted
      `the coroner made an order permitting publication` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner made an order permitting a report (or the part in question) to be published, as desirable in the public interest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May a report of the inquest be published` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the inquest found the death self-inflicted`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 75(5) speaks only after such a finding)
    ELSE IF `the coroner made an order permitting publication` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 79 -- resuming a suspended inquest`

-- s 79(3), (4): if a person has been charged with an indictable offence in which whether they caused the death is in
-- issue, the inquest "may not be resumed ... until the charge is finally determined" (discharged; no further appeal
-- without an extension; or the Attorney General or DPP directs no further proceedings). s 79(5): suspended on the
-- coroner's opinion with no charge, it "may not be resumed until" the Attorney General or DPP advises no proceedings will
-- be taken. s 79(5A): the State Coroner may direct it not be resumed. s 79(2A): no order to resume unless the State
-- Coroner was given written notice naming a day "no earlier than 14 days after the notice is given". "until" and
-- "unless": JUST FALSE while barred; JUST TRUE means the coroner may order it (on the coroner's own motion or on the
-- application of a person with leave, s 79(2)).
@export Can the suspended inquest be resumed yet (s 79)? If you had leave to appear you can apply for it to be resumed (s 79(2)).
GIVEN `why it was suspended` IS A `Why the inquest was suspended` @desc Why the inquest was suspended. One of: "a person was charged with an indictable offence about the death (s 78(1)(a))", "the coroner formed the opinion that a known person committed an indictable offence (s 78(1)(b))"
      `someone has been charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person has been charged with an indictable offence in which whether they caused the death is in issue
      `where the charge stands` IS A `Where the charge stands` @desc If someone was charged: where the charge stands. One of: "not finally determined", "the person was discharged", "no further appeal can be made without an extension of time", "the Attorney General or the DPP directed that no further proceedings be taken"
      `the Attorney General or DPP advised no proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If no one was charged: whether the Attorney General or the Director of Public Prosecutions has advised that no proceedings will be taken against the known person
      `the State Coroner directed it not be resumed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Coroner has directed that the inquest not be resumed (s 79(5A))
      `the day the State Coroner was given notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner gave the State Coroner written notice of the intention to order the inquest resumed (s 79(2A))
      `the day of the proposed order` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the coroner proposes to order the inquest resumed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the suspended inquest be resumed` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the State Coroner directed it not be resumed` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `someone has been charged` AND `where the charge stands` EQUALS `not finally determined` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF     `why it was suspended` EQUALS `the coroner formed the opinion that a known person committed an indictable offence (s 78(1)(b))`
              AND (NOT `someone has been charged`)
              AND (NOT `the Attorney General or DPP advised no proceedings`)
           THEN JUST FALSE
           IF DATE_SERIAL `the day of the proposed order` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL (`The earliest day the inquest may be ordered resumed` `the day the State Coroner was given notice`) THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The one place the s 79(2A) 14 days is written; `Can the suspended inquest be resumed` calls it.
@export What is the earliest day on which the coroner may order the suspended inquest resumed, given when the State Coroner was notified (s 79(2A))?
GIVEN `the day the State Coroner was given notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the coroner gave the State Coroner written notice of the intention to order the inquest resumed
GIVETH A DATE
`The earliest day the inquest may be ordered resumed` MEANS
    DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day the State Coroner was given notice` PLUS 14)

§§ `s 81 -- the findings`

-- s 81(1): at the conclusion or on suspension the coroner records whether the person died and, if so, identity, date
-- and place, and "(c) in the case of an inquest that is being concluded -- the manner and cause of the person's death".
-- s 81(3): the record "must not indicate or in any way suggest that an offence has been committed by any person". On a
-- suspension the manner and cause are not found: JUST FALSE (fork F5).
@export Will the coroner's findings state the manner and cause of death (s 81)? The findings never say or suggest that anyone committed an offence.
GIVEN `the inquest is being concluded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the inquest is being concluded (true) or suspended (false)
      `the person died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the finding is that the person died
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will the findings state the manner and cause of death` MEANS
    IF `the person died` AND `the inquest is being concluded` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `s 83 -- fresh inquests`

-- s 83(5) "An application for a fresh inquest ... may only be made by a police officer or by a person who was granted
-- leave to appear or be represented at a previous inquest". "only": JUST FALSE otherwise.
@export Can I apply to the State Coroner for a fresh inquest (s 83(5))?
GIVEN `I had leave to appear at the previous inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were granted leave to appear, or to be represented, at the previous inquest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for a fresh inquest` MEANS
    IF `I had leave to appear at the previous inquest` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 83(4): "A fresh inquest ... must be held if (a) an application ... is made under this section, and (b) on the basis
-- of the application, the State Coroner is of the opinion that the discovery of new evidence or facts makes it
-- necessary or desirable in the interests of justice". When (4) is not met it requires nothing: JUST FALSE (other
-- powers to hold one, s 83(2), (3), (8), are untouched; fork F6).
@export If I apply, must a fresh inquest be held (s 83(4))? If it must, the State Coroner holds it or directs another coroner to (s 83(6)), and its findings may add to or replace the earlier findings (s 83(7)).
GIVEN `I had leave to appear at the previous inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were granted leave to appear, or to be represented, at the previous inquest
      `I have applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the State Coroner for a fresh inquest
      `the State Coroner considers new evidence makes it necessary or desirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Coroner, on the basis of your application, is of the opinion that the discovery of new evidence or facts makes a fresh inquest necessary or desirable in the interests of justice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must a fresh inquest be held on my application` MEANS
    IF     `I had leave to appear at the previous inquest`
       AND `I have applied`
       AND `the State Coroner considers new evidence makes it necessary or desirable`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 83(2): "A fresh inquest may be held if (a) a previous inquest was terminated before its conclusion because it
-- appeared to the coroner that the person did not die, or (b) a previous inquest was concluded and the coroner's finding,
-- or the jury's recorded verdict, was that the person did not die or that it is uncertain whether the person had died."
-- s 83(3): "If the remains of a person are found in the State, a fresh inquest may be held concerning the death of the
-- person even though a previous inquest was held concerning the suspected death of the person." Without an application
-- (s 83(4) covers one). Outside (2) and (3): JUST FALSE, worded "s 83(2), (3) give no ground on these facts" (fork F15;
-- s 83(4) and (8) keep other routes open).
DECLARE `How the previous inquest ended` IS ONE OF
    `terminated before its conclusion because it appeared the person did not die`
    `concluded with a finding or verdict that the person did not die, or that it is uncertain whether they died`
    `held into a suspected death, and the person's remains have since been found in New South Wales`
    `ended in another way`

@export After a previous inquest, may a fresh inquest be held without an application, because of how it ended or because the remains have been found (s 83(2), (3))? A "no" is about s 83(2) and (3) only: an application under s 83(4), the coroner's other powers (s 83(8)) and the Supreme Court (s 85) remain.
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How the previous inquest ended` @desc How the previous inquest ended; pick the first that fits. One of: "terminated before its conclusion because it appeared the person did not die", "concluded with a finding or verdict that the person did not die, or that it is uncertain whether they died", "held into a suspected death, and the person's remains have since been found in New South Wales", "ended in another way"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May a fresh inquest be held because of how the previous one ended` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
    WHEN `ended in another way` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 84-87 -- the Supreme Court`

-- s 84: on the application of "the Minister or any other person", the Supreme Court may order an inquest "if the Court
-- is satisfied that it is necessary or desirable to do so in the interests of justice", even if one was partly held and
-- terminated or suspended. s 85: likewise an order quashing an inquest and ordering a new one, because of fraud,
-- rejection of evidence, irregularity, insufficiency of inquiry, new evidence "or (f) any other reason". s 86: the
-- applicant must serve notice on the Minister. s 87(3): the coroner "may not dispense with" the inquest ordered.
@export If I apply to the Supreme Court, can it order an inquest, or quash an inquest and order a fresh one (ss 84, 85)? You must serve notice of the application on the Minister (s 86); the Minister may intervene and is entitled to be heard (s 86A); the order must be served on the State Coroner within 21 days (s 87(1)), who then holds the inquest or directs another coroner to (s 87(2)); and a coroner cannot dispense with an inquest the Court orders (s 87(3)).
GIVEN `the Court is satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied that the order is necessary or desirable in the interests of justice (for a fresh inquest, because of fraud, rejected evidence, an irregularity, an insufficient inquiry, new evidence or any other reason)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Supreme Court order an inquest on my application` MEANS
    IF `the Court is satisfied it is necessary or desirable in the interests of justice` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 87(1): "If the Supreme Court makes an order under this Chapter, the order must be served on the State Coroner within
-- 21 days after it is made in accordance with the rules of court of the Court or any directions given by that Court in
-- making the order." The day the order is made is not counted, so the last day is the day made plus 21 (fork F16). The
-- Act does not say who serves; the applicant is asked, since the order follows the applicant's own application. A period
-- the text fixes: served after the last day answers JUST FALSE. The 21 days is written once, in the date function.
@export The Supreme Court made an order on my application (ss 84, 85): by what day must the order be served on the State Coroner (s 87(1))?
GIVEN `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the Supreme Court made the order
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to serve the order on the State Coroner` MEANS
    DATE_FROM_SERIAL (DATE_SERIAL `the day the order was made` PLUS 21)

@export Was the Supreme Court's order on my application served on the State Coroner in time (s 87(1))?
GIVEN `the day the order was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the Supreme Court made the order
      `the day it was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the order was served on the State Coroner
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the order served on the State Coroner in time` MEANS
    IF DATE_SERIAL `the day it was served` AT MOST DATE_SERIAL (`The last day to serve the order on the State Coroner` `the day the order was made`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Part 8.1 -- post mortems, whole organs, exhumation`

-- s 89(6) (and s 25(2) for the inquest): "Without limiting subsection (1), a coroner may decide to dispense with a post
-- mortem examination ... if the coroner is satisfied (after ... consulting with a senior next of kin ...) that (a) the
-- deceased person died of natural causes ... and (b) a senior next of kin ... has indicated to the coroner that it is not
-- the wish of the deceased person's family that a post mortem examination be conducted". "Without limiting": outside
-- these facts the coroner's general discretion remains, so NOTHING.
@export Can the coroner dispense with a post mortem because my family does not want one (s 89(6))? The coroner must be satisfied that the death was from natural causes.
GIVEN `the coroner is satisfied the death was from natural causes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied (after advice from police and doctors and consulting a senior next of kin) that the person died of natural causes, whether or not the precise cause is known
      `a senior next of kin told the coroner the family does not want one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a senior next of kin has told the coroner that the family does not wish a post mortem examination to be conducted
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the coroner dispense with the post mortem at my family's wish` MEANS
    IF     `the coroner is satisfied the death was from natural causes`
       AND `a senior next of kin told the coroner the family does not want one`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text says "includes" in effect: s 89(6) is "without limiting" the coroner's general power in s 89(1))

-- s 90(4): nothing in s 90 authorises keeping whole organs "after a coroner makes an order authorising the disposal of
-- the deceased person's remains unless a coroner makes a further order under subsection (5)". s 90(5): only if the
-- coroner is satisfied it is necessary or desirable for the investigation. s 90(6): notice to a senior next of kin "as
-- soon as is reasonably practicable". Before a disposal order, s 90(4) does not speak: NOTHING.
@export Once the body is released, can the coroner still keep a whole organ (s 90(4), (5))? If a coroner orders one kept, a senior next of kin must be told as soon as reasonably practicable (s 90(6)), and can object (s 96).
GIVEN `a disposal order has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner has made an order authorising the disposal (burial or cremation) of the remains
      `the coroner ordered the organ kept under s 90(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner has made an order under s 90(5) authorising the retention of that specified whole organ
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the coroner keep a whole organ after the body is released` MEANS
    IF (NOT `a disposal order has been made`)
    THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 90(4) speaks only after a disposal order)
    ELSE IF `the coroner ordered the organ kept under s 90(5)` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 91(2): an exhumation warrant "may be issued if" (a) no inquest has been held, (b) it was suspended over an indictable
-- offence, (c) it was terminated on a finding that the person had not died, (d) it was not completed for any other
-- reason, or (e) the Supreme Court quashed it and ordered a fresh one. s 91(3): after (b) or (c), not "unless the inquest
-- may be resumed or a fresh inquest may (or is required to) be held". s 91(1): only if the coroner considers it desirable
-- for a post mortem or a special examination or test.
DECLARE `Where the inquest stands` IS ONE OF
    `no inquest has been held`
    `suspended because of an indictable offence`
    `terminated on a finding that the person had not died`
    `not completed for another reason`
    `quashed by the Supreme Court with a fresh inquest ordered`
    `completed`

@export Can the coroner order the body exhumed (s 91)?
GIVEN `the coroner considers it desirable for an examination or test` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner considers exhumation desirable to direct a post mortem or a special examination or test (or a further or more complete one)
      `where the inquest stands` IS A `Where the inquest stands` @desc Where any inquest into the death stands. One of: "no inquest has been held", "suspended because of an indictable offence", "terminated on a finding that the person had not died", "not completed for another reason", "quashed by the Supreme Court with a fresh inquest ordered", "completed"
      `the inquest may be resumed or a fresh one held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the inquest was suspended or terminated: whether it may be resumed, or a fresh inquest may (or must) be held under the Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the coroner order the body exhumed` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the coroner considers it desirable for an examination or test`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE CONSIDER `where the inquest stands`
         WHEN `completed` THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `suspended because of an indictable offence` THEN `only if it can go on`
         WHEN `terminated on a finding that the person had not died` THEN `only if it can go on`
         OTHERWISE JUST TRUE
    WHERE
        `only if it can go on` MEANS
            IF `the inquest may be resumed or a fresh one held` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 94: "Anything done or omitted to be done by a medical practitioner or other person does not subject the person to
-- any action, liability, claim or demand if the act or omission was done ... in good faith for the purposes of"
-- conducting a directed post mortem, examination or test, or a review of medical records. A flat "does not": JUST
-- FALSE. Outside its "if", s 94 does not speak: NOTHING (the general law governs).
@export Can I bring a claim against the doctor (or other person) for what was done in a post mortem, examination, test or records review the coroner directed (s 94)?
GIVEN `it was done in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission you complain of was done in good faith
      `it was done for the directed examination, test or review` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was done for the purposes of conducting a post mortem, examination or test, or a review of medical records, under a direction under the Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I claim against the post mortem doctor` MEANS
    IF `it was done in good faith` AND `it was done for the directed examination, test or review`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (outside the scope: s 94 bars claims only for good-faith acts for those purposes; the general law decides the rest)

§§ `Part 8.2 -- objecting to a post mortem`

-- s 96(1): "A senior next of kin ... may, by notice in writing, request a coroner ... not to exercise" a relevant post
-- mortem investigative function (s 95: directing a post mortem; authorising the retention of whole organs). s 96(5):
-- the coroner may refuse a senior next of kin's request about a post mortem if satisfied that person "has been, or may
-- be, charged with an offence in connection with the ... death". s 98: a senior next of kin may authorise another person
-- in writing, who acts on giving the coroner a copy of the authority -- but not after a s 96(5) refusal (s 98(3)).
-- s 99: anyone else may object, but "The other provisions of this Part do not apply".
@export Can I object to a post mortem examination (or to a whole organ being kept), and which route do I have (ss 96, 98, 99)?
GIVEN `I am a senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a senior next of kin of the person who died (see the "senior next of kin" question)
      `I hold a senior next of kin's written authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a senior next of kin has authorised you in writing to act for them under Part 8.2, and you will give the coroner a copy
      `that senior next of kin's own request was refused under s 96(5)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you hold an authority: whether the senior next of kin who gave it had their own objection to a post mortem refused because they have been or may be charged
      `the coroner is satisfied I have been or may be charged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that you have been, or may be, charged with an offence in connection with the death
      `it is about a post mortem` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your objection is to a post mortem examination (true) or to the retention of a whole organ (false)
GIVETH A `How I can object`
`How can I object to the post mortem` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I am a senior next of kin`
           THEN IF `the coroner is satisfied I have been or may be charged` AND `it is about a post mortem`
                THEN `as senior next of kin, by written notice, but the coroner may refuse it because I have been or may be charged`
                ELSE `as senior next of kin, by written notice: ss 96 and 97 apply`
           IF     `I hold a senior next of kin's written authority`
              AND (NOT (`that senior next of kin's own request was refused under s 96(5)` AND `it is about a post mortem`))
           THEN `as the senior next of kin's authorised person, by written notice with a copy of my authority: ss 96 and 97 apply`
           OTHERWISE `only as another person, under s 99: the 48-hour notice and the Supreme Court route do not apply`

-- s 97(1): "A senior next of kin to whom a notice has been given under section 96(3) may apply to the Supreme Court within
-- 48 hours after the notice was given". s 97(2): the application "operates to stay" the coroner's direction or order.
-- Written as a rule: the senior next of kin must apply within 48 hours (counted in hours from the notice). Jurally this
-- is a power that lapses, not a duty; the rule models the lapse as LEST BREACH. An application at exactly 48 hours is
-- within (fork F7). An application made after the 48 hours is read as not a s 97(1) application, so it gives no s 97(2)
-- stay (fork F18).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Post mortem party` `Post mortem act`
`s 97 -- the senior next of kin must apply to the Supreme Court within 48 hours of the notice` MEANS
    PARTY `the senior next of kin`
    MUST `apply to the Supreme Court`
    WITHIN 48
    LEST BREACH BY `the senior next of kin` BECAUSE "no application to the Supreme Court within 48 hours after the s 96(3) notice (s 97(1))"

GIVEN `hours after the notice when I applied` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `applied in time` `hours after the notice when I applied` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 97 -- the senior next of kin must apply to the Supreme Court within 48 hours of the notice` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `hours after the notice when I applied`), (EVENT `the senior next of kin` `apply to the Supreme Court` `hours after the notice when I applied`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export The coroner has told me in writing that the post mortem (or organ retention) I objected to will go ahead: is my application to the Supreme Court in time to stop it for now (s 97)?
GIVEN `I was given a s 96(3) notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner gave you, as senior next of kin who objected, written notice that the post mortem or organ retention is necessary or desirable (s 96(3))
      `hours after the notice when I applied` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after you were given that notice you applied (or will apply) to the Supreme Court
GIVETH A `My Supreme Court application`
`Is my Supreme Court application in time` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I was given a s 96(3) notice`)
    THEN `section 97 does not arise: no section 96(3) notice was given to me`
    ELSE IF `applied in time` `hours after the notice when I applied`
         THEN `in time: the coroner's direction or order is stayed until the Court decides`
         ELSE `out of time: the 48 hours after the notice have passed`

-- ss 96(3), (4)(a), 97(2), (3)(a): after the senior next of kin's objection the coroner must decide; if the post mortem
-- is necessary or desirable, written notice must "indicate the earliest time at which the post mortem examination may be
-- conducted (being a time that is not earlier than 48 hours after the senior next of kin has been given the notice)"; an
-- application to the Supreme Court within 48 hours stays the direction; the Court may order that it not be conducted, be
-- conducted within limitations, or confirm the direction. Before the coroner decides, the Act does not say the post
-- mortem must wait, but s 96(3) presupposes it (fork F8).
@export I objected to the post mortem as senior next of kin: can it go ahead now (ss 96, 97)?
GIVEN `a senior next of kin objected in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or another senior next of kin, or their authorised person) asked the coroner in writing not to direct the post mortem
      `the coroner gave written notice it will go ahead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided the post mortem is necessary or desirable and gave the objecting senior next of kin written notice of that decision
      `hours since the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since that notice was given
      `hours after the notice the notice says it may start` IS A NUMBER @desc The earliest time the notice says the post mortem may be conducted, in hours after the notice was given (the Act requires at least 48)
      `I applied to the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a senior next of kin given the notice has applied to the Supreme Court for an order that the post mortem not be conducted
      `hours after the notice when I applied` IS A NUMBER @desc If so, how many hours after the notice the application was made
      `the Supreme Court order` IS A `Supreme Court order on my application` @desc What the Supreme Court has ordered. One of: "no order yet", "an order that the post mortem not be conducted", "an order that it be conducted within limitations", "an order confirming the coroner's direction"
GIVETH A `Can the post mortem go ahead`
`Can the post mortem go ahead now` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `a senior next of kin objected in writing`) THEN `nothing in Part 8.2 holds it back: no senior next of kin objected`
           IF (NOT `the coroner gave written notice it will go ahead`) THEN `not yet: the coroner must first decide on my objection and give me written notice`
           IF `the Supreme Court order` EQUALS `an order that the post mortem not be conducted` THEN `no: the Supreme Court ordered that it not be conducted`
           IF `the Supreme Court order` EQUALS `an order that it be conducted within limitations` THEN `yes, but only within the limitations the Supreme Court set`
           IF `the Supreme Court order` EQUALS `an order confirming the coroner's direction` THEN `yes`
           IF `I applied to the Supreme Court` AND `applied in time` `hours after the notice when I applied`
           THEN `not while my Supreme Court application is pending: it is stayed`
           IF    `hours since the notice` LESS THAN 48
              OR `hours since the notice` LESS THAN `hours after the notice the notice says it may start`
           THEN `not yet: the time the notice gives (at least 48 hours after it) has not come`
           OTHERWISE `yes`

§§ `ss 100, 101 -- burial, cremation and removal`

-- s 100(3): an appropriate disposal authorisation is (a) for a person other than a stillborn child: a doctor's notice
-- under BDM Act s 39, a coroner's order under s 101, or a Registrar's certificate under BDM Act s 51; (b) for a stillborn
-- child: a doctor's certificate or notice under BDM Act s 12(3), or a coroner's order under s 101.
GIVEN `the document` IS A `Disposal document`
      `stillborn` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an appropriate disposal authorisation` `the document` `stillborn` IF
    CONSIDER `the document`
    WHEN `a coroner's order under s 101` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a doctor's notice under s 39 of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act` THEN (NOT `stillborn`)
    WHEN `a Registrar's certificate under s 51 of that Act` THEN (NOT `stillborn`)
    WHEN `a doctor's certificate or notice under s 12(3) of that Act (stillborn child)` THEN `stillborn`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 100(1): "A person must not bury or cremate human remains, or place [them] in a mausoleum or other permanent resting
-- place ... unless (a) the person has been given, or has in his or her possession, an appropriate disposal
-- authorisation ... or (b) the disposal ... is otherwise authorised by the regulations." s 100(2): must not hand remains
-- over for anatomical or medical research, or remove them "(other than cremated remains)" from the State, "unless (a) an
-- appropriate disposal authorisation ... has been issued" or the regulations authorise it. Max penalty 50 penalty units
-- each. A flat "must not ... unless": JUST TRUE when the "unless" is met, JUST FALSE otherwise. Cremated remains taken
-- out of NSW are outside s 100(2): NOTHING (fork F9).
@export May I bury or cremate the body, hand it over for research, or take it out of NSW (s 100)? Doing so without authority carries a maximum penalty of 50 penalty units.
GIVEN `what I want to do` IS A `What I want to do with the remains` @desc What you want to do. One of: "bury or cremate them, or place them in a permanent resting place", "hand them over for anatomical or medical research", "take them out of New South Wales (not cremated remains)", "take cremated remains out of New South Wales"
      `the document issued` IS A `Disposal document` @desc The disposal document issued for these remains, if any. One of: "a doctor's notice under s 39 of the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act", "a doctor's certificate or notice under s 12(3) of that Act (stillborn child)", "a coroner's order under s 101", "a Registrar's certificate under s 51 of that Act", "no document"
      `I have it or was given it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have that document in your possession or have been given it
      `the remains are of a stillborn child` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the remains are those of a stillborn child
      `the regulations authorise it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations under the Act otherwise authorise this disposal (not deposited here)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I bury, cremate or remove the remains` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what I want to do`
    WHEN `take cremated remains out of New South Wales` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a limit in the text's own wording: s 100(2) excludes cremated remains and says nothing more; fork F9)
    WHEN `bury or cremate them, or place them in a permanent resting place` THEN
        IF (`authorised` AND `I have it or was given it`) OR `the regulations authorise it` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE
        IF `authorised` OR `the regulations authorise it` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `authorised` MEANS `is an appropriate disposal authorisation` `the document issued` `the remains are of a stillborn child`

§§ `Maximum penalties`

GIVEN pu IS A NUMBER, mo IS A NUMBER, dy IS A NUMBER, both IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
`penalty of` pu mo dy both MEANS `Maximum penalty` WITH
    `penalty units` IS pu
    `months of imprisonment` IS mo
    `days of imprisonment` IS dy
    `fine and imprisonment both` IS both

-- The maximum penalties for an individual, from each offence's own "Maximum penalty" line: ss 35(2), 53(5), 62: 10
-- penalty units; s 44: 100 penalty units or 2 years, or both; ss 74(3), 75(7), 76: 10 penalty units or 6 months (for an
-- individual; 50 penalty units otherwise); s 100(1), (2): 50 penalty units; s 103A: 14 days or 10 penalty units, or
-- both. The dollar value of a penalty unit is set by another Act (Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999, s 17), not
-- deposited: the answer is in penalty units.
@export What is the most I can be fined or imprisoned for an offence under the Coroners Act? The fine is in penalty units (their dollar value is set by the Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999).
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc The offence. One of: "failing to report a reportable death (s 35(2))", "obstructing a person executing a coronial investigation scene order (s 44)", "failing to comply with a notice to produce (s 53(5))", "refusing to be sworn, examined, answer or produce (s 62)", "contravening an order clearing the court or forbidding publication (s 74(3))", "publishing in breach of a self-inflicted-death order or s 75(5) (s 75(7))", "publishing a disallowed question, warning, objection or submission (s 76)", "burying or cremating remains without authority (s 100(1))", "handing over or removing remains without authority (s 100(2))", "disrespectful behaviour in coronial proceedings (s 103A)"
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
`What is the maximum penalty` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `obstructing a person executing a coronial investigation scene order (s 44)` THEN `penalty of` 100 24 0 TRUE
    WHEN `contravening an order clearing the court or forbidding publication (s 74(3))` THEN `penalty of` 10 6 0 FALSE
    WHEN `publishing in breach of a self-inflicted-death order or s 75(5) (s 75(7))` THEN `penalty of` 10 6 0 FALSE
    WHEN `publishing a disallowed question, warning, objection or submission (s 76)` THEN `penalty of` 10 6 0 FALSE
    WHEN `burying or cremating remains without authority (s 100(1))` THEN `penalty of` 50 0 0 FALSE
    WHEN `handing over or removing remains without authority (s 100(2))` THEN `penalty of` 50 0 0 FALSE
    WHEN `disrespectful behaviour in coronial proceedings (s 103A)` THEN `penalty of` 10 0 14 TRUE
    OTHERWISE `penalty of` 10 0 0 FALSE
